title: Vaginal Bleeding
---

# Vaginal Bleeding

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Per vaginal bleeding</u> (PV bleeding) - a presenting problem detected by the patient, but which may be of gynaecological origin (further classified into lower and upper genital tract causes), or non-gynaecological origin (PR bleeding, haematuria) that is mistakened by patients as PV bleeding
  - <u>Abnormal uterine bleeding</u> (AUB) - bleeding that is **assumed to arise from the uterus**
- **Etiology of PV bleeding:**
  - **Pregnancy-related** - early pregnancy complications (ectopic pregnancy, miscarriage), late pregnancy complications
  - **Non pregnancy-related:**
    - <u>Lower-genital tract bleeding</u> - cervical bleeding, vaginal bleeding, vulvar bleeding
    - <u>Upper-genital tract bleeding</u> - abnormal uterine bleeding, fallopian tube bleeding, ovarian bleeding
    - <u>Non-genital tract disease</u> (mistakened as PV bleeding) - PR bleeding, haematuria or skin conditions involving the perineum or skin beyond the vulva
- **Lower genital tract bleeding:**
  - **Cervical bleeding** - often presents w/ <u>sporadic spotting</u>, and <u>post-coital bleeding</u>, and the cause is easily identifiable on speculum examination:
    - <u>Benign lesions</u> - cervical polyps, cervical ectropion, pelvic organ prolase (ulcerations), cervicitis (strawberry red spots if Trichomonas vaginalis)
    - <u>Malignant lesions</u> - cervical cancer, direct extension from other gynaecological cancers (e.g. endometrial carcinoma)
  - **Vaginal bleeding** - presents similar as cervical bleeding w/ <u>sporadic spotting</u>, and <u>post-coital bleeding</u>, however trauma-associated vaginal bleeding can cause major haemorrhage:
    - <u>Trauma</u>:
      - Intercourse-related - e.g. coitarche (tearing of intact hymen), sexual assault
      - Non-intercourse-related - e.g. pelvic trauma, straddle-type injuries
    - <u>Infection/ inflammation</u> - vaginitis, vaginal ulcers
    - <u>Benign growths</u> - vaginal polyps, gartner duct cysts, vaginal adenosis
    - <u>Atrophic vaginitis</u> (i.e. genitourinary syndrome) - a/w hypoestrogenic changes
    - <u>Malignant growths</u> - primary vaginal cancer
  - **Vulvar bleeding** - often presents w/ <u>sporadic bleeding</u>, but can be directly visualised on infection:
    - <u>Benign lesions</u> - STI, candida vulvovaginitis, condylomata acuminatum etc.
    - <u>Malignant lesions</u> - primary vulvar cancer
- **Upper genital tract bleeding:**
  - <u>Uterine bleeding</u> - clinical presentation of AUB is extremely variable, but is a deviation of normal menstrual cycle (by frequency, regularity, duration, flow, intermenstrual bleeding or unscheduled bleeding), and is classified by FIGO PALM-COEIN system (see below)
  - <u>Fallopian tube bleeding</u> - rarely presenting as vaginal bleeding but are caused by <u>tubal pregnancy</u>, infections (PID), malignancies
  - <u>Ovarian bleeding</u> - rarely presenting as vaginal bleeding (more commonly intra-peritoneal bleeding), but theoretically possible via the fallopian tube
- **Workup** - see section on AUB
